A load test measures the maximum requests per second a single instance can handle while meeting your latency SLA. Run Gatling or k6 against a production-like environment (not a development laptop with a local database), gradually increasing concurrent users until p99 latency exceeds the SLA threshold or CPU utilization crosses 80%.

```javascript
// k6 load test with staged ramp-up
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check } from 'k6';

export const options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '2m', target: 50 },   // warm up
    { duration: '5m', target: 200 },   // steady load
    { duration: '5m', target: 500 },   // find the ceiling
  ],
  thresholds: {
    http_req_duration: ['p(99)<200'],  // p99 must stay under 200ms
  },
};

export default function () {
  const res = http.get('https://staging-api.example.com/products');
  check(res, { 'status 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
}
```

The staged ramp reveals the ceiling precisely. At 50 concurrent users the service handles 300 RPS with p99 at 50ms. At 200 users it reaches 450 RPS with p99 at 120ms. At 500 users p99 jumps to 800ms and errors climb — the ceiling sits between 200 and 500 concurrent users. Bisect in follow-up tests to narrow the exact breaking point.

The critical detail: test the full request path including database queries, cache lookups, and downstream calls. A load test that hits a mock service measures the wrong bottleneck — HTTP throughput is rarely the limiting factor. The database usually is.
